<h2>CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION</h2>
<h3>1.1 OVERVIEW OF FOOD WASTE AND REDISTRIBUTION</h3>
<p>Food waste is a multidisciplinary problem affecting economies and environments globally. Traditional food donation relies heavily on manual coordination, phone calls, and physical inspection of food quality. The growing demand for sustainable food practices has highlighted the need for scalable software solutions capable of automating and validating food donations safely.</p>

<h3>1.2 WHY TECHNOLOGY FOR FOOD DONATION</h3>
<p>Modern web technology offers a compelling solution to the logistical challenges of food recovery. By providing a centralized, real-time dashboard, donors and volunteers can seamlessly coordinate pickups. Integrating AI for preliminary food safety checks reduces the risk of redistributing spoiled food, ensuring high standards of public health.</p>

<h3>1.3 CURRENT CHALLENGES</h3>
<p>Hardware accessibility is no longer a primary barrier, but system complexity remains high. Coordinating live, moving volunteers in a city requires real-time data streaming (WebSockets). Furthermore, ensuring that users can intuitively navigate dashboards requires responsive, high-fidelity UI/UX design.</p>

<h3>1.4 ROLE OF MODERN WEB STACKS</h3>
<p>Modern frameworks like React and Node.js have democratized real-time application development. Combined with state managers like Zustand and mapping libraries like React-Leaflet, they provide a comprehensive framework for building live-tracking dashboards and dynamic UI elements.</p>

<h3>1.5 SCOPE OF THIS PROJECT</h3>
<p>FoodCovering focuses on a streamlined logistics scenario: a donor uploads food, an AI assesses quality via visual analysis, and if approved, a volunteer is routed for pickup. The project supports both desktop and mobile web views and encompasses a full front-end dashboard, live tracking map, simulated AI analysis, and system health reporting.</p>

<hr>

<h2>CHAPTER 2: LITERATURE SURVEY</h2>
<h3>2.1 SURVEY</h3>
<p>In recent years, smart-city logistics have been increasingly adopted to combat food waste. Research shows that digital platforms can enhance volunteer engagement and reduce transit times for perishable goods. Investigation into AI-based computer vision for food spoilage demonstrates that visual indicators (color, texture) can effectively predict shelf life. FoodCovering builds upon this research by implementing a fully immersive web-based dashboard that not only tracks logistics but embeds simulated AI safety checks directly into the donation pipeline.</p>

<hr>

<h2>CHAPTER 3: REQUIREMENTS ANALYSIS</h2>
<h3>3.1 OBJECTIVE OF THE PROJECT</h3>
<p>The primary objectives span five dimensions:<br>
- <strong>Educational/Social:</strong> To provide a platform that safely redistributes food to those in need.<br>
- <strong>Logistical:</strong> To track and route volunteers in real-time across a city map.<br>
- <strong>Safety:</strong> To utilize AI image analysis to prevent the distribution of spoiled food.<br>
- <strong>Technical:</strong> To demonstrate the feasibility of building a real-time web application using Vite, React, Node.js, and Socket.io.<br>
- <strong>Analytical:</strong> To aggregate system data into comprehensive reports and charts.</p>

<h3>3.2 REQUIREMENT ANALYSIS</h3>
<p><strong>A. Hardware Requirements</strong><br>
- Processor: Intel Core i5 or equivalent<br>
- RAM: 8 GB (16 GB Recommended)<br>
- Storage: 1 GB free space<br>
- OS: Windows 10/11 or macOS</p>

<p><strong>B. Software Requirements</strong><br>
- Environment: Node.js, npm<br>
- Frontend: React 18, Vite, TailwindCSS, React-Leaflet, Recharts<br>
- Backend: Express, Socket.io, TSX<br>
- Language: TypeScript</p>

<hr>

<h2>CHAPTER 4: DESIGN DESCRIPTION OF PROPOSED PROJECT</h2>
<h3>4.1 PROPOSED METHODOLOGY</h3>
<p>The development followed an iterative approach:<br>
1. <strong>Scene/UI Construction:</strong> Building the layout using Tailwind CSS.<br>
2. <strong>Dashboard Systems:</strong> Implementing Recharts for data visualization.<br>
3. <strong>AI Analysis Simulation:</strong> Creating the drag-and-drop image upload with scanning animations.<br>
4. <strong>Live Pipeline:</strong> Setting up a Node.js Socket.io server to broadcast live coordinates and metrics.<br>
5. <strong>Map Integration:</strong> Embedding Leaflet to render the real-time movement of users.</p>

<h3>4.1.1 SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE</h3>
<p>The system is divided into a Client-Server model. The Frontend (React/Zustand) acts as the display layer, ingesting data. The Backend (Node.js) acts as the simulation engine, mutating coordinates, generating random metrics, and pushing payloads via WebSockets to all connected clients.</p>

<h3>4.1.2 VARIOUS STAGES</h3>
<p>1. <strong>Evidence/Donation Upload:</strong> Donor uploads a food image.<br>
2. <strong>AI Processing:</strong> The system scans the image and evaluates spoilage risk.<br>
3. <strong>Dispatch:</strong> A volunteer is assigned.<br>
4. <strong>Live Tracking:</strong> The map updates as the volunteer moves to the donor and then to the facility.<br>
5. <strong>Completion:</strong> The dashboard metrics update to reflect the successful delivery.</p>

<h3>4.1.3 INTERNAL / COMPONENT DESIGN</h3>
<p>The UI is modular. Dashboard.tsx aggregates KPI cards. FoodAnalysis.tsx handles file reading and visual feedback. Tracking.tsx and MapView.tsx consume the cityMap arrays from the global Zustand store to plot Leaflet markers.</p>

<h3>4.1.4 WORKING PRINCIPLES</h3>
<p>The application utilizes WebSocket event-driven architecture. The server runs a setInterval loop every 3 seconds, updating coordinates slightly to simulate movement, and modifying server health metrics, then emits a pipelineData event. The client listens to this event and merges it into the React state.</p>

<h3>4.2 NOVELTY OF THE PROJECT</h3>
<p>FoodCovering's novelty lies in:<br>
1. <strong>Integrated AI Workflow:</strong> Combining food safety directly with logistics.<br>
2. <strong>Real-time Map Visualization:</strong> Active live tracking of all moving parts on a city-wide scale.<br>
3. <strong>High-Fidelity Dashboard:</strong> Enterprise-grade data visualization for charity operations.</p>

<hr>

<h2>CHAPTER 5: RESULTS AND DISCUSSION</h2>
<h3>5.1 DEVELOPMENT ENVIRONMENT</h3>
<p>Developed using VS Code with Vite for fast HMR. The backend runs on a local Node instance using tsx. The application renders fluidly in modern web browsers.</p>

<h3>5.2 ASSET INTEGRATION</h3>
<p>- <strong>UI Assets:</strong> Lucide React icons.<br>
- <strong>Mapping:</strong> OpenStreetMap tiles.<br>
- <strong>Charts:</strong> Recharts SVG components.</p>

<h3>5.3 SCRIPT INVENTORY AND METRICS</h3>
<p>- App.tsx: Route management and Socket initialization.<br>
- usePipelineStore.ts: Global state management for live data.<br>
- index.ts (Backend): Simulation engine and socket broadcaster.<br>
- Dashboard/Tracking/MapView.tsx: Core UI views.</p>

<h3>5.4 FEATURE EVALUATION</h3>
<p>The system successfully processes mock AI uploads locally via FileReader, accurately overlays scanning visuals, and efficiently plots moving data points on the map without rendering bottlenecks.</p>

<hr>

<h2>CHAPTER 6: SUMMARY AND CONCLUSION</h2>
<h3>6.1 SUMMARY</h3>
<p>FoodCovering successfully demonstrates the viability of modern web technologies to manage complex, real-time humanitarian logistics. The project delivers a fully interactive dashboard featuring authentic tracking, reporting, and automated food safety checks.</p>

<h3>6.2 CONCLUSION</h3>
<p>By combining immersive UI, real-time data flow, and simulated AI logic, the project bridges the gap between theoretical logistics and practical execution. It proves that software can effectively automate the safety and distribution of donated food, protecting public health while minimizing waste.</p>

<h3>6.3 FUTURE ENHANCEMENT</h3>
<p>Future versions could integrate true Machine Learning models (like TensorFlow.js) for actual image recognition, real GPS tracking via mobile apps for volunteers, and SMS integration via Twilio for automated dispatch alerts.</p>

